<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hans Christian Andersen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1875">1875</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hans Christian Andersen was a Danish writer, born in Odense, best known for his fairy tales, among them &quot;The Little Mermaid&quot;, &quot;The Ugly Duckling&quot;, &quot;The Emperor's New Clothes&quot; and &quot;The Snow Queen&quot;. He also wrote novels, plays, poems and travel books. His tales, published from 1835, have been translated into more languages than almost any other works of literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hans-christian-andersen.jpg" alt="Hans Christian Andersen" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thora Hallager · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HCA_by_Thora_Hallager_1869_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hans Christian Andersen was born in Odense on 2 April 1805. His father, also named Hans, had an elementary school education and introduced his son to literature by reading him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">Arabian Nights</a>; his mother, Anne Marie Andersdatter, was an illiterate washerwoman. After his father's death in 1816 the boy attended a local school for poor children and had to support himself, working as an apprentice to a weaver and later to a tailor.</p><p>At 14 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> to seek work as an actor and was accepted into the Royal Danish Theatre for his soprano voice, which soon broke. A colleague told him he considered him a poet, and Andersen turned to writing. Jonas Collin, the theatre's director, persuaded King Frederick VI to pay part of his schooling at Slagelse and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Elsinore</a>, which lasted until 1827.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first story, &quot;The Ghost at Palnatoke's Grave&quot;, appeared in 1822. In 1829 he had considerable success with the short story &quot;A Journey on Foot from Holmen's Canal to the East Point of Amager&quot;, followed by a play and a short volume of poems. A small travel grant from the king in 1833 let him begin the first of many journeys through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in October 1834. His travels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> shaped his first novel, The Improvisatore, a fictionalised autobiography published in 1835 to instant acclaim.</p><p>Andersen's first fairy tales were issued in three booklets by C. A. Reitzel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> between May 1835 and April 1837. The first, of 8 May 1835, held &quot;The Tinderbox&quot;, &quot;Little Claus and Big Claus&quot;, &quot;The Princess and the Pea&quot; and &quot;Little Ida's Flowers&quot;; the first three were based on folktales he had heard in childhood. In June 1847 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a>. In 1857 he stayed five weeks at Dickens's home, Gads Hill Place, after which Dickens stopped writing to him, which Andersen never understood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In early 1872 Andersen fell out of his bed and was severely hurt, and soon afterwards he showed signs of liver cancer. He died on 4 August 1875 at Rolighed, a country house near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> belonging to his friends Moritz G. Melchior and his wife. By then he was internationally revered, and the Danish government paid him an annual stipend as a national treasure. His 156 fairy tales in nine volumes have been translated into more than 125 languages and have inspired ballets, plays and films.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hans Christian Andersen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Danish figures of wide influence include the philosopher Søren Kierkegaard, the writer <b>Hans Christian Andersen</b> and the physicist Niels Bohr, whose Copenhagen institute became a centre of quantum physics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Odense, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Rolighed, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.4,10.38&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Odense on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4777 272 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hans Christian Andersen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5227 542h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5227 542h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.4,10.38&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My life is a lovely story, happy and full of incident.&#8221;</p><footer>The True Story of My Life (1847), opening words &#183; tr. Mary Howitt, 1847 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7007/pg7007-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The history of my life will say to the world what it says to me—There is a loving God, who directs all things for the best.&#8221;</p><footer>The True Story of My Life (1847) &#183; tr. Mary Howitt, 1847 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7007/pg7007-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Madame, politics are like Venus; they whom she decoys into her castle perish.&#8221;</p><footer>The True Story of My Life (1847) &#183; tr. Mary Howitt, 1847 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7007/pg7007-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
